IMPORT prelude

-- Income Tax Assessment Act 1997 (Commonwealth of Australia) -- how much income tax I owe: taxable income (s 4-15), the
-- method statement (s 4-10), ordinary income (s 6-5), general deductions (s 8-1), deductible gifts (s 30-15, limited by
-- s 26-55), tax losses (ss 36-10, 36-15, 36-17), the Low Income tax offset (ss 61-110, 61-115) and the working Australians
-- tax offset (ss 61-155, 61-160); with the rates brought in from the Income Tax Rates Act 1986 (s 3 tax-free threshold,
-- Schedule 7 Part I, s 23(2), s 23AA).
--
-- SEAT: the taxpayer -- an individual Australian resident ("How much income tax must I pay?"). Where the Act taxes a company
-- the individual owns (Jarrah Legal Technologies Pty Ltd: its rate, its losses), the question is asked by the founder and
-- shareholder ("What rate does my company pay?"); the company is the entity the Act addresses, and that is a departure
-- recorded in NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 267, compilation date 27 August 2026 (includes amendments up to Act No. 79, 2026; register id
-- C2026C00400, 12 volumes), as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A05138.txt. Rates: Income Tax Rates Act 1986,
-- Compilation No. 66, compilation date 1 July 2026 (includes amendments up to Act No. 50, 2026; register id C2026C00300), as
-- deposited at ../../../income-tax-rates-act-1986/registers/source-bundle/C2004A03348.txt.
--
-- NOT ENCODED. Part-year residents and the pro-rated threshold (Rates Act s 20, formula not in the deposit); non-residents,
-- working holiday makers, minors (Div 6AA), superannuation and employment termination remainders; the Medicare levy and HELP
-- repayments (other Acts); what counts as ordinary income or a business (case law -- asked as facts); every tax offset other
-- than the two named; the trustee limbs of Div 61; the continuity-of-ownership and business-continuity tests for company
-- losses (Subdivs 165-A, 165-B); gifts of property, the named-recipient tables of Subdiv 30-B and the spreading of a gift
-- deduction over up to 5 years (Subdiv 30-DB); the application date of Subdiv 61-E (in the amending Act No. 49, 2026, not in
-- the compilation -- asked as a fact).
--
-- A year of income is named by the calendar year in which it begins: 2024 is the 2024-25 income year.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written with the writing-l4-rules skill from the individual's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No
-- pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Income Tax Assessment Act 1997 -- income tax`

§§ `Helpers`

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lesser of` MEANS
    IF `a` AT MOST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the greater of` MEANS
    IF `a` AT LEAST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

-- The part of `amount` that lies above `from`, up to `width` dollars of it.
GIVEN `amount` IS A NUMBER, `from` IS A NUMBER, `width` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the slice` MEANS
    IF `amount` AT MOST `from` THEN 0
    ELSE `the lesser of` (`amount` MINUS `from`) `width`

-- A width large enough to stand for "exceeds $190,000" with no upper limit.
`no upper limit` MEANS 1000000000000

§§ `4-15 -- taxable income`

-- s 4-15(1) Step 3: "Subtract your deductions from your assessable income (unless they exceed it). The result is your taxable
-- income. (If the deductions equal or exceed the assessable income, you don't have a taxable income.)" Nil stands for "no
-- taxable income".
@export What is my taxable income for the income year (s 4-15)?
GIVEN `assessable income` IS A NUMBER @desc All your assessable income for the income year, in dollars
      `deductions` IS A NUMBER @desc All your deductions for the income year, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is my taxable income` MEANS
    `the greater of` (`assessable income` MINUS `deductions`) 0

§§ `Income Tax Rates Act 1986 s 3 and Schedule 7 Part I -- the resident rates`

-- Rates Act s 3(1): "tax-free threshold means $18,200."
`the tax-free threshold` MEANS 18200

-- Schedule 7 Part I clause 1(b): "for each part of the ordinary taxable income specified in the table applicable to the year of
-- income -- the rate applicable under that table". Three tables only: "for the 2024-25 or 2025-26 year of income" (item 1,
-- "exceeds the tax-free threshold but does not exceed $45,000" -- 16%), "for the 2026-27 year of income" (15%) and "for the
-- 2027-28 year of income or a later year of income" (14%). Items 2 to 4 are the same in all three: over $45,000 to $135,000 --
-- 30%; over $135,000 to $190,000 -- 37%; over $190,000 -- 45%. The deposited compilation has no table for 2023-24 or earlier,
-- so for those years the answer is NOTHING: not covered by this deposit (c045 asks about 2023-24).
GIVEN `year` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the item 1 rate in percent` MEANS
    IF `year` LESS THAN 2024 THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `year` AT MOST 2025 THEN JUST 16
         ELSE IF `year` EQUALS 2026 THEN JUST 15
              ELSE JUST 14

GIVEN `item 1 rate` IS A NUMBER, `income` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the resident table tax` MEANS
        `the slice` `income` `the tax-free threshold` (45000 MINUS `the tax-free threshold`) TIMES `item 1 rate` DIVIDED BY 100
    PLUS `the slice` `income` 45000 90000 TIMES 30 DIVIDED BY 100
    PLUS `the slice` `income` 135000 55000 TIMES 37 DIVIDED BY 100
    PLUS `the slice` `income` 190000 `no upper limit` TIMES 45 DIVIDED BY 100

§§ `4-10 Step 2 -- basic income tax liability`

-- s 4-10(3) Step 2: "Work out your basic income tax liability on your taxable income using: (a) the income tax rate or rates
-- that apply to you for the income year". For a resident for the whole year, with no superannuation or employment termination
-- remainder and no working holiday income, the ordinary taxable income is the taxable income (Rates Act s 3) and Schedule 7
-- Part I applies. NOTHING: the deposit has no rates for that year.
@export What is my basic income tax liability before offsets, as a full-year resident (s 4-10 Step 2; Rates Act Schedule 7)?
GIVEN `year` IS A NUMBER @desc The calendar year in which the income year begins (2024 for 2024-25)
      `taxable income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your taxable income for the income year, in dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is my basic income tax liability` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the item 1 rate in percent` `year`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST `r` THEN JUST (`the resident table tax` `r` `taxable income`)

§§ `61-110, 61-115 -- Low Income tax offset`

-- s 61-110(1): entitled for 2020-21 or a later income year if "(a) you are an individual who is an Australian resident at any
-- time during the income year; and (b) your taxable income for the income year does not exceed $66,667."
-- s 61-115(1) table: "does not exceed $37,500 -- $700"; "exceeds $37,500 but is not more than $45,000 -- $700, less an amount
-- equal to 5% of the excess"; "exceeds $45,000 but is not more than $66,667 -- $325, less an amount equal to 1.5% of the excess".
@export What Low Income tax offset do I get (ss 61-110, 61-115)?
GIVEN `resident at any time in the year` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were an Australian resident at any time in the income year
      `taxable income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your taxable income for the income year, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What Low Income tax offset do I get` MEANS
    IF (NOT `resident at any time in the year`) OR `taxable income` GREATER THAN 66667 THEN 0
    ELSE IF `taxable income` AT MOST 37500 THEN 700
         ELSE IF `taxable income` AT MOST 45000 THEN 700 MINUS (`taxable income` MINUS 37500) TIMES 5 DIVIDED BY 100
              ELSE `the greater of` (325 MINUS (`taxable income` MINUS 45000) TIMES 15 DIVIDED BY 1000) 0

§§ `61-155, 61-160 -- working Australians tax offset`

-- s 61-155(1): entitled if an Australian resident at any time in the year and "the amount worked out for you under subsection
-- (2) (about your net labour income) exceeds the tax-free threshold (within the meaning of the Income Tax Rates Act 1986)".
-- s 61-160: "the lesser of: (a) $250; and (b) the amount that would be the amount of your basic income tax liability for the
-- income year if your taxable income was comprised only of your net labour income". Subdiv 61-E was inserted by Act No. 49,
-- 2026; the year it first applies is in that Act, not in the compilation, so it is asked as a fact. The notional liability
-- uses the resident table for the year; NOTHING if the deposit has none.
@export What working Australians tax offset do I get (ss 61-155, 61-160)?
GIVEN `the offset applies to the year` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether Subdivision 61-E applies to this income year (set by the amending Act No. 49, 2026)
      `resident at any time in the year` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were an Australian resident at any time in the income year
      `year` IS A NUMBER @desc The calendar year in which the income year begins
      `net labour income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your net labour income under s 61-155(2): wages, sole-trader business income, personal services income and ESS discounts, less the deductions against them
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What working Australians tax offset do I get` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the offset applies to the year` AND `resident at any time in the year` AND `net labour income` GREATER THAN `the tax-free threshold`)
    THEN JUST 0
    ELSE CONSIDER `What is my basic income tax liability` `year` `net labour income`
         WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
         WHEN JUST `liability` THEN JUST (`the lesser of` 250 `liability`)

§§ `4-10 Steps 3 and 4 -- the income tax I owe`

-- s 4-10(3) Step 4: "Subtract your *tax offsets from your basic income tax liability. The result is how much income tax you owe".
-- s 63-10 table item 20, "Any *tax offset not covered by another item": "You cannot get a refund of it, you cannot transfer it
-- and you cannot carry it forward" (item 3 gives the working Australians offset the same treatment), so neither offset takes the
-- tax owed below nil. A full-year resident is assumed; the Medicare levy and HELP repayments are other Acts.
@export How much income tax do I owe as a full-year resident, after the Low Income and working Australians offsets (s 4-10)?
GIVEN `year` IS A NUMBER @desc The calendar year in which the income year begins
      `taxable income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your taxable income for the income year, in dollars
      `the working Australians offset applies to the year` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether Subdivision 61-E applies to this income year
      `net labour income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your net labour income under s 61-155(2), in dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`How much income tax do I owe` MEANS
    CONSIDER `What is my basic income tax liability` `year` `taxable income`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST `basic` THEN
        CONSIDER `What working Australians tax offset do I get` `the working Australians offset applies to the year` TRUE `year` `net labour income`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        WHEN JUST `wato` THEN
            JUST (`the greater of` (`basic` MINUS `What Low Income tax offset do I get` TRUE `taxable income` MINUS `wato`) 0)

§§ `6-5 -- ordinary income`

-- s 6-5(2): a resident's assessable income includes ordinary income "derived directly or indirectly from all sources, whether
-- in or out of Australia"; (3) a foreign resident's includes ordinary income from "all *Australian sources". Whether an amount
-- is ordinary income at all (wages, a sole trader's takings) is case law, asked as a fact.
@export Is this ordinary income part of my assessable income (s 6-5)?
GIVEN `Australian resident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were an Australian resident when you derived it
      `from an Australian source` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the income has an Australian source
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is this ordinary income assessable to me` IF
    `Australian resident` OR `from an Australian source`

§§ `8-1 -- general deductions`

-- s 8-1(1): deductible "to the extent that (a) it is incurred in gaining or producing your assessable income; or (b) it is
-- necessarily incurred in carrying on a *business"; (2) not "to the extent that (a) it is a loss or outgoing of capital, or of a
-- capital nature; or (b) ... of a private or domestic nature; or (c) ... incurred in relation to gaining or producing your
-- *exempt income or your *non-assessable non-exempt income; or (d) a provision of this Act prevents you from deducting it."
-- Whether a course fee is incurred in gaining current income (c083) is a question of fact, so each limb is asked.
DECLARE Outgoing HAS
    `incurred in gaining or producing assessable income` IS A BOOLEAN
    `necessarily incurred in carrying on a business` IS A BOOLEAN
    `capital or of a capital nature` IS A BOOLEAN
    `private or domestic` IS A BOOLEAN
    `relates to exempt or non-assessable non-exempt income` IS A BOOLEAN
    `another provision prevents the deduction` IS A BOOLEAN

@export Can I deduct this outgoing under the general deduction rule (s 8-1)?
GIVEN `the outgoing` IS AN Outgoing @desc The expense you incurred, described by the s 8-1 limbs
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I deduct this outgoing` IF
        (`the outgoing`'s `incurred in gaining or producing assessable income` OR `the outgoing`'s `necessarily incurred in carrying on a business`)
    AND (NOT `the outgoing`'s `capital or of a capital nature`)
    AND (NOT `the outgoing`'s `private or domestic`)
    AND (NOT `the outgoing`'s `relates to exempt or non-assessable non-exempt income`)
    AND (NOT `the outgoing`'s `another provision prevents the deduction`)

§§ `30-15, 26-55 -- deductible gifts`

-- s 30-15(1) table item 1: a gift of "money" to "A fund, authority or institution covered by an item in any of the
-- tables in Subdivision 30-B" -- you can deduct "the amount you are giving"; item 2 does the same for an *ancillary fund (a
-- private ancillary fund, as in d072). Recipient status (endorsed or named; in Australia, s 30-17) is asked as one fact.
-- s 30-15(2): "A testamentary gift or contribution is not deductible under this section."
-- s 26-55: the total of Division 30 deductions is limited to assessable income less all other deductions (except tax losses).
@export Can I deduct this gift of money, before the yearly limit (s 30-15)?
GIVEN `money given` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of money you gave, in dollars
      `recipient is a deductible gift recipient or ancillary fund` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the recipient is covered by s 30-15 item 1 or 2 (a deductible gift recipient or an ancillary fund)
      `testamentary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the gift is made by will
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much of my gift is deductible` MEANS
    IF `recipient is a deductible gift recipient or ancillary fund` AND NOT `testamentary` THEN `money given` ELSE 0

@export How much of my gift can I deduct this income year after the s 26-55 limit?
GIVEN `money given` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of money you gave, in dollars
      `recipient is a deductible gift recipient or ancillary fund` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the recipient is covered by s 30-15 item 1 or 2
      `testamentary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the gift is made by will
      `assessable income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your assessable income for the year, in dollars
      `other deductions` IS A NUMBER @desc Your deductions other than gifts and tax losses, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much of my gift can I deduct this year` MEANS
    `the lesser of` (`How much of my gift is deductible` `money given` `recipient is a deductible gift recipient or ancillary fund` `testamentary`)
                    (`the greater of` (`assessable income` MINUS `other deductions`) 0)

§§ `36-10, 36-15, 36-17 -- tax losses`

-- s 36-10: add up the deductions (except earlier tax losses), "(2) Subtract your total assessable income. (3) If you *derived
-- *exempt income, also subtract your *net exempt income ... (4) Any amount remaining is your tax loss".
@export What is my tax loss for the year (s 36-10)?
GIVEN `deductions` IS A NUMBER @desc Your deductions for the year other than earlier tax losses, in dollars
      `assessable income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your assessable income for the year, in dollars
      `net exempt income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your net exempt income for the year, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is my tax loss` MEANS
    `the greater of` (`deductions` MINUS `assessable income` MINUS `net exempt income`) 0

-- s 36-15(2), an individual with no net exempt income: "If your total assessable income for the later income year exceeds your
-- total deductions (other than *tax losses), you deduct the tax loss from that excess." No choice.
@export How much of my earlier tax loss must I deduct this year (s 36-15)?
GIVEN `this year's assessable income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your assessable income this year, in dollars
      `this year's deductions` IS A NUMBER @desc Your deductions this year other than tax losses, in dollars
      `undeducted loss` IS A NUMBER @desc Your earlier tax losses not yet deducted, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much of my earlier loss must I deduct` MEANS
    `the lesser of` `undeducted loss` (`the greater of` (`this year's assessable income` MINUS `this year's deductions`) 0)

-- s 36-17(2), a corporate tax entity with no net exempt income: "the entity is to deduct from that excess so much of the tax loss
-- as the entity chooses. The entity may choose a nil amount." Asked by the founder about the company (d04a).
@export How much of its earlier tax loss may my company deduct this year (s 36-17)?
GIVEN `this year's assessable income` IS A NUMBER @desc The company's assessable income this year, in dollars
      `this year's deductions` IS A NUMBER @desc The company's deductions this year other than tax losses, in dollars
      `undeducted loss` IS A NUMBER @desc The company's earlier tax losses not yet deducted, in dollars
      `amount chosen` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount the company chooses to deduct (it may choose nil)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much of its loss may my company deduct` MEANS
    `the lesser of` (`the lesser of` `amount chosen` `undeducted loss`)
                    (`the greater of` (`this year's assessable income` MINUS `this year's deductions`) 0)

§§ `Income Tax Rates Act 1986 ss 23(2), 23AA -- my company's rate`

-- Rates Act s 23AA: "An entity is a base rate entity for a year of income if: (a) no more than 80% of its assessable income for
-- the year of income is base rate entity passive income; and (b) its aggregated turnover ... is less than $50 million."
-- s 23(2): "(a) if the company is a base rate entity for a year of income -- 25%; or (b) otherwise -- 30%".
@export What rate of income tax does my company pay, in percent (Rates Act ss 23(2), 23AA)?
GIVEN `assessable income` IS A NUMBER @desc The company's assessable income for the year, in dollars
      `base rate entity passive income` IS A NUMBER @desc The part of it that is passive income under Rates Act s 23AB, in dollars
      `aggregated turnover` IS A NUMBER @desc The company's aggregated turnover for the year, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What rate does my company pay` MEANS
    IF `base rate entity passive income` AT MOST `assessable income` TIMES 80 DIVIDED BY 100 AND `aggregated turnover` LESS THAN 50000000
    THEN 25
    ELSE 30
